WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.306
Dijkstra's algorithm finds the shortest distance from one start node

2
00:00:04.406 --> 00:00:07.448
to every other node. Here the start node is A.

3
00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:11.340
The numbers on the edges are lengths, and each edge works in both

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.048
directions. Every length must be zero or more.

5
00:00:14.148 --> 00:00:17.876
Each node has an estimate: the shortest length found so far.

6
00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:20.274
At the start, A has estimate zero.

7
00:00:20.374 --> 00:00:24.186
Every other node has estimate infinity: no path found yet.

8
00:00:24.286 --> 00:00:26.008
Then repeat two steps.

9
00:00:26.108 --> 00:00:29.772
First, settle the unsettled node with the smallest estimate.

10
00:00:29.872 --> 00:00:33.386
A settled estimate is final: it is the node's distance.

11
00:00:33.486 --> 00:00:37.051
We check why below. Second, relax its edges.

12
00:00:37.151 --> 00:00:40.766
To relax an edge, check whether the path through the settled node is

13
00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:41.305
shorter.

14
00:00:41.405 --> 00:00:45.209
If it is, keep the shorter estimate, and remember where it came from.

15
00:00:46.898 --> 00:00:51.002
Settle A. From A, B is four away, and C is two away.

16
00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:54.935
The smallest estimate is now C, at two. Settle C.

17
00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:57.397
Through C, B is two plus one: three.

18
00:00:57.497 --> 00:01:00.243
That is shorter than four, so B becomes three.

19
00:01:00.343 --> 00:01:04.774
D becomes two plus eight: ten. E becomes two plus ten: twelve.

20
00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:08.731
This is why the algorithm settles the smallest estimate, and not the

21
00:01:08.831 --> 00:01:10.110
first node it reached.

22
00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:14.048
Had it settled B at four, as soon as A reached it, the answer would be

23
00:01:14.148 --> 00:01:15.942
wrong: the path through C is three.

24
00:01:17.834 --> 00:01:20.175
B has the smallest estimate, three.

25
00:01:20.275 --> 00:01:22.116
Can a path found later still beat three?

26
00:01:22.801 --> 00:01:26.016
Any path to B starts in the settled nodes, A and C.

27
00:01:26.116 --> 00:01:28.500
Follow it to the first node outside them.

28
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.647
If that node is B, B's estimate already counts the path: three.

29
00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:37.393
If it is D or E, the path so far costs at least D's estimate, ten, or

30
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:41.189
E's, twelve, because A and C have relaxed their edges.

31
00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:44.624
No length is negative, so the rest of the path can only add.

32
00:01:44.718 --> 00:01:48.582
So no other path can cost less than three. Settle B.

33
00:01:48.682 --> 00:01:50.608
Its estimate never changes again.

34
00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:57.354
Now relax B's edges. Through B, D is three plus five: eight.

35
00:01:57.454 --> 00:01:58.925
Eight is shorter than ten:

36
00:01:59.025 --> 00:02:02.474
the bound of ten held only for paths that leave A and C at D.

37
00:02:02.568 --> 00:02:06.666
D, at eight, is now the smallest estimate. Settle D.

38
00:02:06.766 --> 00:02:11.518
E becomes eight plus two: ten. F becomes eight plus six: fourteen.

39
00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:14.548
Pause here and predict. D is settled.

40
00:02:14.648 --> 00:02:17.172
Which node is settled next, and at what distance?

41
00:02:20.268 --> 00:02:24.089
E, at ten. It is the smallest unsettled estimate.

42
00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:27.404
Through E, F becomes ten plus three: thirteen.

43
00:02:27.504 --> 00:02:28.940
That is shorter than fourteen.

44
00:02:29.034 --> 00:02:32.100
Settle F, at thirteen. Every node is settled.

45
00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:35.805
Each node remembers the neighbor that gave its final distance.

46
00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:39.483
Follow them back from F: E, D, B, C, and then A.

47
00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:44.505
So the shortest path to F is A, C, B, D, E, F. Its length is thirteen.

48
00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:48.578
Why must every length be zero or more?

49
00:02:48.678 --> 00:02:53.241
Here is a small graph with one negative edge, from C to B, of length

50
00:02:53.341 --> 00:02:56.395
minus two. Here each edge works in one direction only.

51
00:02:56.495 --> 00:03:00.619
Settle A. From A, B is two away, and C is three away.

52
00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:04.809
B has the smallest estimate, so the algorithm settles B at two.

53
00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:05.961
It is now final.

54
00:03:06.061 --> 00:03:10.800
Then it settles C, at three. Through C, B costs three minus two: one.

55
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.425
That is shorter, but B is already final. The algorithm answers two.

56
00:03:15.525 --> 00:03:16.940
The true distance is one.

57
00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.024
With negative lengths, use the Bellman-Ford algorithm instead.
